.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt DEFAULT-GATEWAY 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm default-gateway
.Nd print the gateway of the default route
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl 4 | Fl 6
.Op Fl is
.Op Fl f Ar fib
.Nm
.Fl h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
prints the gateway of the default route, IPv4 unless
.Fl 6 ,
asking the routing table the way each system answers best:
.Ic route -n get default
on
.Fx ,
.Ic ip route show default
on Linux.
The first route listed is the one in use, and the one reported.
.Pp
A default route with no gateway, through a tunnel interface say, prints
that interface in the gateway's place, in both columns with
.Fl i ,
so the output always has the same columns.
An IPv6 link-local gateway is printed with its
.Ql % Ns Ar interface
scope on both systems, so the output can be used as it is: Linux leaves
the scope out, and it is added.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-f fib"
.It Fl 4
The IPv4 default route; the default.
.It Fl 6
The IPv6 default route.
.It Fl f Ar fib
Look in this FIB on
.Fx ,
or in this routing table on Linux, given by its number.
.It Fl h
Print the usage and exit.
.It Fl i
Print the outgoing interface after the gateway, separated by a space.
.It Fl s
Print no trailing newline.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
A default route was found.
.It 1
There is no default route of that family, or the platform is neither
.Fx
nor Linux.
.It 2
A usage error, a
.Ar fib
that is not a number included.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
The IPv4 gateway, and the interface it is reached by:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ default-gateway -i
192.0.2.1 em0
.Ed
.Pp
The IPv6 gateway in FIB 1:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ default-gateway -6 -f 1
fe80::1%em0
.Ed
.Pp
Ping it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ping -c 1 "$(default-gateway)"
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr setfib 1 ,
.Xr ip 8 ,
.Xr route 8
.Sh CAVEATS
Only the first nexthop of a multipath route is reported.
